      {
        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "I'm sketching out the capybara's proportions—roughly 1.2m long, chubby and rounded for a whimsical feel—plus a small cheerful songbird perched on its head, considering a soft cartoony palette rather than a strict toon-kit style.\n\nI'll skip looking at other samples and instead focus on the design directly: a barrel-shaped body, oversized blunt-snouted head, tiny round ears, blush cheeks, whiskers, and stubby legs, exaggerated for a storybook cuteness. For eyes I'll draw a custom glossy round SVG decal with a highlight rather than using a preset. Overall height including the bird on top comes to about 0.65m.\n\nNow I'm working out the skeleton: a root hip joint at the rump, a spine chain running forward through chest joints toward a short neck and head, keeping the proportions matched to the chubby whimsical body I just sketched out.\n\nI'm working out the spine chain points and naming convention for the joints, then sketching the body loft stations — figuring out the barrel length, width, and height so the capybara's belly sits appropriately low given its stubby legs.\n\nAdding more loft stations...\n\nNow I'm thinking through the head shape — a boxy capybara skull tapering to a blunt snout, built as its own loft from skull to nose, with a joint connecting it to the body angled slightly downward and forward.\n\nI want upper and lower jaw as separate pieces: the lower jaw a small loft beneath the mouth with a forward joint, plus two tiny orange buck teeth peeking out for whimsy. Then perched on top of the head between where the ears would be, I'm adding a small round songbird about 0.1 m tall, like a chickadee.\n\nFor the bird itself I'll give it its own simple joint chain (root, head, upper/lower beak, wings, tail) attached to the head joint. I'm picturing it as a bluebird with a blue back, rusty-orange breast, and white belly to contrast nicely against the capybara's warm tan-brown fur — small ellipsoid body, sphere head, orange beak.\n\nFor details I'm adding black eyes with a highlight, swept-back wings, a three-feather tail fan, thin twig legs gripping the capybara's fur with little toes, and a tiny crest feather for charm. I'll keep the storytelling simple — just the bird perched on the head, maybe with a small daisy tucked behind the capybara's ear as a cute accessory, without overloading the scene with extra elements like flowers held by the bird or a nest.\n\nNow working out the capybara's legs: each limb has shoulder, elbow, wrist, and foot joints, with four toes in front and three toes on the hind feet, plus small webbed nails. I'm structuring each leg as a multi-segment chain with varying thickness (thicker near the shoulder, tapering toward the paw), and placing the paws with toe rings and tiny claws. I'm positioning the shoulder joints out at the wider sides of the body since the torso narrows toward the bottom center.\n\nNow I'm working out joint placements for both front and hind legs, using distance checks against segment-length sums to make sure the bends look natural -- front legs bending back at the elbow, hind legs bending forward at the knee and back at the hock, with the last segment kept roughly vertical. I want the hind legs slightly longer than the front, like a real capybara, with the rump sitting a touch higher for a whimsical proportion.\n\nI'm also designing the feet: flattened pad capsules at the tips, plus short toe capsules (three for hind, four for front) spreading forward from the foot, with dark nail-like tips, and thinking through whether to rig the toes as optional digit joints.\n\nFor the rest, I'm planning a tiny tail stub joint since capybaras barely have one, and small rounded ears set high on the head with a pinkish inner shape, deciding whether to build them as flattened spheres or a simple extrusion.\n\nThen for eyes, I'm thinking about glossy, slightly oversized eyes with a custom decal texture capturing that sleepy, relaxed capybara look—dark iris, highlights, and a drooping lid line for the chill expression.\n\nFor the nose, I'll add a blunt dark-brown rhinarium cap at the snout tip with small nostril dents, keeping it naturalistic rather than pink.\n\nAdding whiskers, two small orange incisors peeking from the upper lip, and sketching the shaggy reddish-brown coat with countershaded belly and whimsical fur tufts on the back, cheeks, and chest.\n\nI'm leaning toward skipping the cel-shading toon effect since it risks double shadows on top of the painterly mottled fur—sticking with warm cinnamon tones for the coat palette instead.\n\nNow I'm working out the full cast's colors: capybara with cinnamon back, dark brown belly markings, orange teeth; the bird gets blue plumage with an orange breast and amber beak; the daisy keeps its classic white-petal yellow-center look. I'm also considering adding a soft pink blush decal on the cheeks using a radial gradient for a whimsical touch, though I need to watch out for the alpha cutoff clipping the gradient transparency.\n\nThinking about the blush more, I settle on a flat pinkish-orange ellipse with three thin anime-style stripe marks instead of a gradient, keeping the flat color count low since paint materials don't count against the limit. I decide to skip adding ground or grass, but I'll place a small flattened-sphere daisy flower near the ear using a ring of eight petals attached to a head bone. Now I'm checking the loft function parameters for width, height, and station settings.\n\nI plan to use loft for both body and head, and for a bird perched on the capybara's head I use a raycast down from above to find where it hits the crown so the bird's feet land correctly there, scaling the bird to about 11% of body length and parenting its joints to the head. I'm now laying out the head joint position and aim direction with a slight downward tilt, then working through the loft station coordinates (z, y, width, height) that define the skull shape from back to front.\n\nI'm checking the resulting proportions -- head top around y=0.615 versus the body top around 0.565, which is a bit tall but gives a whimsical big-headed look that's fine to keep, and with the bird added overall height reaches roughly 0.72. Comparing this to a real capybara's head length of about 0.25, my current stations span 0.30 to 0.70 which is close enough to proceed.\n\nNow I'm setting up the neck joint connecting the spine end to the head, considering whether to name the chain with just one joint or split it into \"neck\" and \"head\" segments so the tip becomes a separate head joint.\n\nLaying out the hips root joint and spine chain with specific coordinates, then extending into the neck path toward the head base. I'm checking whether loft can accept a combined bone list of hips, spine chain, and neck chain, since the body loft path needs to run through all station centers via catmull interpolation.\n\nNow I'm drafting the actual station list—z-position, y-height, width, and height values—for the hindquarters and rump area, trying to match the rounder profile of a capybara's body silhouette while keeping it roughly aligned with the spine bones.\n\nI should also check the loft function's signature to confirm it can accept these stations with a rounded cap at z=-0.60, and start planning the fur details—tufts at the belly, chest, cheeks, and back, using a grain texture for streaking and some countershading paint to give it a whimsical, soft appearance.\n\nDefining coat and head paint gradients...\n\nI'm placing a dark bulging nose pad ellipsoid near the snout tip with slit-shaped nostrils on its top-front surface, then positioning small orange boxy incisors just below it for the upper lip and starting work on the lower jaw caps.\n\nNow I'm rethinking the head-jaw split: rather than treating the lower jaw as hanging below a full head loft, I need to shorten the upper head's height so the lower jaw fills the remaining volume underneath, keeping the jaw joint and chin positions consistent with the head's bottom profile. I'm reworking the head loft stations to reflect this shorter upper section.\n\nI'm thinking the mouth line should hide where jaw meets upper head, with a dark seam sweeping upward at the corners for a whimsical smile effect, curving forward via a catmull spline on each side. I'm also considering adding puffy cheek spheres to round out the whimsical look, though overall head proportions seem reasonable as is.\n\nNow I'm placing the ears at the top-back of the head, with a small bird perched between them at the crown, and widening the head slightly near z=0.45. I'm debating whether to add a flower too — maybe a tiny daisy tucked behind one ear — but worried the head is getting crowded, so I might save that detail for later or move it elsewhere on the capybara's body.\n\nI'm leaning toward having the bird sing with its beak slightly open, paired with a sleepy, content capybara with half-closed eyes, to give the scene a gentle story. For the bird's body I'm thinking of a capsule-like sweep with colored bands, anchored near the crown and facing forward toward the viewer.\n\nSetting up bird joint hierarchy...\n\nThen for the body shape I'm scaling a small sphere into an ellipsoid and painting it with a countershading gradient—blue on the back, orange breast, white belly—based on normal direction relative to the bird's facing vector.\n\nNow I'm adding the head as another small sphere placed at the front-top of the body, blue with hints of white around the eyes, plus tiny black eye spheres with white highlight dots, and a two-part orange beak (upper cone and a lower cone posed open via a jaw joint rotation). For the wings I'm building a teardrop extrusion shape positioned against the body sides, painted blue with darker wingtips and white wing-bar accents...\n\nFor the tail I'm extruding three tapered feather slabs fanned out from the rear at slight angles, then adding thin twig-like legs with three forward toes, a tiny tuft crest of three spikes on the head, and scaling the whole bird to about 0.13m long and 0.1m tall to match the earlier head proportions.\n\nNow I'm turning to capybara details: toenails, webbing, paw pads, inner ear, eye and nose decals, whiskers, cheek fluff, tiny eyebrows, chin shading, and the characteristic dark oval scent-gland bump (morrillo) on the snout bridge, plus a sagging belly for cuteness. I'm setting up coat color variables for the fur palette...\n\nStill defining fur blotching...\n\nI'm darkening the lower legs and toes with a gradient, setting pink ear interiors with a light fur rim, and planning fur tuft textures as grayscale SVG shapes so they can be tinted dynamically by the coat color paint at the root level.\n\nNow I'm figuring out card scatter counts for body, cheek, and chest tufts with varying sizes and lean angles, keeping triangle counts efficient, and adding dark curved whiskers extending from the muzzle sides.\n\nFor the feet, I'm shaping rounded ellipsoid pads and four small capsule toes with tiny nail cones, positioning each joint along the foot and ankle.\n\nNow I'm building the leg chains, setting the front legs to parent from the spine's last joint and the hind legs from the hip root, starting the joint path at the shoulder offset from midline, then sweeping fur-colored geometry along each chain with tapered radii from thigh to ankle.\n\nI want the leg sweeps to embed into the body smoothly, so I'm painting them with a coat gradient that darkens toward the lower leg, adding a small stub tail joint off the back, and planning whimsical touches like round cheeks and sleepy eyes with blush decals. Before finishing the code, I'm checking the builder's loft and decal function signatures to make sure I call them correctly.\n\n",
